# Intermediate CA certificates for chain COMPLETION only. Never trust anchors.
#
# Why this file exists (2026-09-17): egazette.gov.in serves ONLY its leaf
# certificate. Browsers fetch the missing issuer from the leaf's Authority
# Information Access URL; Python's ssl does not, so every verified fetch failed
# "unable to get local issuer certificate" and checker.robots failed closed --
# which also blocked the Gazette watcher.
#
# Chain: egazette.gov.in -> YR2 -> Root YR -> ISRG Root X1 (already trusted).
# Both certificates below were fetched from the AIA URLs named in the chain
# itself, and the chain was checked with OpenSSL before they were committed:
#   openssl verify -CAfile <certifi> -untrusted <this file> <leaf>   -> OK
#   openssl verify -CAfile <this file> <leaf>                        -> FAILS
# The second line is the safety property: these alone anchor nothing. The chain
# must still end at a self-signed root from the trusted bundle, and
# checker/robots.py explicitly clears VERIFY_X509_PARTIAL_CHAIN so that stays true
# on every Python version.
#
# When these expire (YR2: 2028-09-02, Root YR cross-sign: 2032-09-02), the
# eGazette fetch will fail closed again. checker/robots.py's _test() reports the
# earliest expiry so that is visible in advance.
#
# subject= /C=US/O=Let's Encrypt/CN=YR2
# issuer= /C=US/O=ISRG/CN=Root YR
# source: http://yr2.i.lencr.org/  fetched 2026-09-17
# notAfter=Sep  2 23:59:59 2028 GMT
